{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"fab-dismal-haddock","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## The finding: an outage we cannot explain\n\nAt 21:28 UTC on 2026-09-04 the Gerrit host stopped serving. Ten alarms fired correctly within\nseven minutes. Service was restored by an operator-authorised stop/start. **The cause is unknown\nand is now unrecoverable.**\n\nThat is the finding. Not the outage — the fact that a full production outage produced no\nexplanation.\n\n## Why nothing could be learned, enumerated\n\n**1. There is NO off-box log destination.** A live AWS audit earlier the same evening enumerated\n15 CloudWatch log groups in the account; ALL belong to unrelated projects (`lmn-*`, `snap-demo-*`).\n**There is no log group for the rebar Gerrit host.** Its logs live only in on-box journald and are\nlost with the host. The 2026-09-02 five-hour outage had the same gap; nothing changed between the\ntwo incidents.\n\n**2. EC2 console output is not a substitute.** Fetched during the incident it returned bytes\ntimestamped **2026-09-02T14:17:32** — two days stale. It is cached and refreshes on its own\nschedule, so it is unreliable as an incident diagnostic. Zero OOM/panic/IO-error lines, which\nproves nothing about tonight.\n\n**3. The telemetry is SELF-REPORTED BY THE THING BEING OBSERVED.** Every `rebar/host` metric is\npublished by `observability.sh` running on the host. When the host stops, observation stops with\nit. The last datapoint (21:28) is not \"the last healthy reading before failure\" — it is simply\nwhere the recording ends. We know the box went quiet; we have no witness to what happened in the\nseconds after.\n\nThis is the structural point. The dead-man alarm work (`bff5`) is CORRECT and did its job: it\nconverted silence into a page. But \"the host is gone\" is where the information ends, and there is\nno path from that alarm to a cause.\n\n**4. Recovery destroys evidence, forcing a false choice.** With no off-box logs, the only forensic\nstate was in the box's own memory and journald. Restarting to restore service destroys it. So\nevery incident presents as: restore quickly, or diagnose — never both. That tradeoff is an\nartefact of the missing telemetry, not a law of nature.\n\n## What we could NOT rule in or out tonight\n\nThe leading hypothesis was memory exhaustion under heavy concurrent gate load — plausible because\na prior audit measured ~9.6 GB of demand against an 8 GiB host when reviews stack, `deploy_deferrals`\nran to 69 in 24h, and `mem_available_percent` swung 36-57% in the preceding 90 minutes. It remains\n**UNPROVEN**, and with the box restarted it is now unprovable for this incident.\n\nNote also that `mem_available_percent` is **measurement-only — nothing alarms on it** (tracked on\n`witted-invisible-roan`). So even the leading suspect had no detector.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 - Host logs survive the host. journald (at minimum: kernel, docker, nginx, and the rebar\n      services) ships off-box with a defined retention. Prove it by terminating a log source and\n      showing its last lines are readable from off-box afterwards.\n- [ ] AC2 - There is at least one observation of host health that does NOT originate on the host,\n      so a dead host cannot silence its own observer. Today every `rebar/host` signal is\n      self-reported; a synthetic/external probe closes that. Prove it by making the host stop\n      publishing and showing the external observation still reports.\n- [ ] AC3 - After an unexplained outage, a documented path exists from \"an alarm fired\" to\n      \"here is what the box was doing\", WITHOUT requiring the box to still be alive. Demonstrate\n      it against tonight's incident window as far as retained data permits, and state plainly\n      which questions remain unanswerable.\n- [ ] AC4 - Restoring service no longer destroys the only evidence. Either the evidence is already\n      off-box by then (AC1), or an explicitly documented capture step precedes restart and is short\n      enough not to meaningfully delay recovery. State the expected added downtime.\n- [ ] AC5 - Defect-seeded: a check fails if log shipping silently stops. A logging pipeline that\n      quietly dies recreates this exact gap while appearing configured — the same\n      absence-of-execution-reported-as-success class that has recurred repeatedly in this repo.\n\n## Explicit non-goals\n\nNot fixing the outage cause (unknown, and separately tracked once known). Not adding a memory\nalarm — that belongs to `witted-invisible-roan` and should not be duplicated here, though this\nticket is more evidence for it. Not building a full observability platform: the bar is \"a future\nincident can be explained\", not \"we have dashboards\".\n\n## Cost note\n\nRetention is a real cost and the account already carries 15 log groups with `retentionInDays: None`\n(never expire) from other projects. Whatever is chosen here should set an explicit retention rather\nthan inheriting that pattern.","id":"9018-3385-ccfa-4568","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"A full production outage produced no diagnosable evidence: no off-box logs, stale console output, and telemetry self-reported by the host that dies with it"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788559581870607001,"uuid":"3f86ddfc-9f97-4650-9185-4a9357ddd2ab"}